window.docContents[17].resolve({"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Stokes-and-Navier___Stokes-Flow--Exact-Planar-Flows--The-Taylor___Green-vortex":{"contents":"The planar Taylor–Green vortex\n\n\n\nis divergence free and solves the Navier–Stokes momentum equations with unit\nviscosity at every . This is\nthe Taylor–Green momentum identity together with\nthe Taylor–Green incompressibility identity.\n\nThe streamfunction is , so the vorticity is\n. Since  is a function of ,\n, and the nonlinear term in the momentum equation is\na gradient, balanced by . What is left is , which\nholds because  is a Laplacian eigenfunction with eigenvalue . In Lean\neach partial derivative is computed by its own lemma and the residuals then\nreduce by cas.\n\n\n\nThe streamlines in the figure do not move. Every Fourier mode of the flow has\n, so viscosity scales the whole pattern by  and the energy\nin a period square falls as . The vortex was introduced by\n as initial data for the\nthree-dimensional energy cascade; in the plane the same field is an exact\nsolution.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009Stokes and Navier–Stokes Flow\u0009Exact Planar Flows","header":"8.5.2.1. The Taylor–Green vortex","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Stokes-and-Navier___Stokes-Flow--Exact-Planar-Flows--The-Taylor___Green-vortex"},"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior--Finite-Time-Growth___-Lifespan___-and-Similarity--Equation-Specific-Branches":{"contents":"The nonlinear heat branch begins on a bounded domain, where a first\nDirichlet eigenfunction moment excludes nonnegative weak solutions with large\ninitial data on a prescribed interval. On the whole space it contains a\ncomplete example: a nonnegative spatially constant classical solution is\nverified directly and its spatial supremum norm is proved to blow up at finite\ntime. The same chapter derives Type I and scaling-invariant profile equations.\nThese profile theorems say when an ansatz solves the PDE and when its scale\nforces concentration; the profile itself is a hypothesis.\n\nThe parabolic Keller–Segel branch derives the density and potential profile\nequations under the standard similarity scaling. A pair of  profiles\nsatisfying both equations, with nonnegative density profile, generates a\nclassical pre-singular solution. A bounded density profile with one nonzero\nvalue makes the density ansatz blow up in the spatial supremum norm.\n\nThe virial branch follows a different route. An affine second-moment identity\nand nonnegativity constrain the right endpoint of a solution interval by an\nexplicit horizon. This is a lifespan theorem: it rules out continuation of a\nsolution retaining the stated moment properties beyond that time.\n\nThe abnormal three-dimensional scaling is analyzed as an obstruction\ncalculation. Its amplitude diverges and its diffusion coefficient vanishes\nin rescaled variables, but the physical residual can diverge. Under the\naggregation profile equation, the exact criterion says that the density\nresidual vanishes everywhere precisely when the density profile is harmonic.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Finite-Time Behavior\u0009Finite-Time Growth, Lifespan, and Similarity","header":"6.7.1.3. Equation-Specific Branches","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior--Finite-Time-Growth___-Lifespan___-and-Similarity--Equation-Specific-Branches"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Using-Verified-Computation--Numerical-Integration":{"contents":"An exact primitive is often unnecessary for a numerical estimate. The Gaussian integral\n, for example, can be bounded directly:\n\nexample :\n    |(∫ x in (0 : ℝ)..1, Real.exp (-x ^ 2)) - 746824 / 1000000| ≤ 1 / 1000000 := by\n  cega\n\n\nOn a panel , Simpson's approximation is\n\n\n\nIf  is four times continuously differentiable there and , then\n. CEGA bounds the fourth derivative and each of the\nthree sample values. It adds the sample uncertainty and the analytic remainder to obtain\nan interval containing the integral, then adds the intervals from adjacent panels.\nCompiled code doubles the number of panels from one until the stated bound holds and then\nchooses the leanest working precision for the elementary function enclosures;\n(panels := n) fixes the panel count and (digits := d) the precision. Both errors are\nincluded in the proved bound.\n\nAt a corner or a nonsmooth endpoint, Simpson's hypotheses may fail even though the integral\nexists. If continuity can be proved, the tactic can instead bound the range on each panel:\n implies . This route applies to\n at zero and to  across zero, though its bounds can require more subdivision.\nThe automatic frontend requires a closed real expression and rational limits.\nFailure to prove the requested accuracy is not a claim that the integral does not exist.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Using Verified Computation","header":"10.5.5. Numerical Integration","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Using-Verified-Computation--Numerical-Integration"}});